				<div class="post-738 post hentry category-os category-performance category-powershell category-virtual-pc">
			<h2 id="post-738"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/2009/07/1-reason-to-stick-with-windows-virtual-pc/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link to 1 reason to stick with Windows Virtual PC">1 reason to stick with Windows Virtual PC</a></h2>
			<small class="meta">July 10th, 2009 </small>

			<div class="entry">
				<p>For those who aren’t aware, Windows 7 (which I absolutely love) has the ability to mount and run native Virtual PC VHD’s.&#160; This is huge, but it <a href="http://www.hanselman.com/blog/LessVirtualMoreMachineWindows7AndTheMagicOfBootToVHD.aspx" target="_blank">isn’t exactly a cake walk</a>.&#160; That is until now!&#160; <a href="http://devhawk.net/#" target="_blank">DevHawk</a> has <a href="http://devhawk.net/2009/06/18/AddBcdVhdps1.aspx" target="_blank">provided a PowerShell script</a> to make life beautiful.&#160; Never complain about your Virtual machine performance again… get (all but disk) native performance.</p>
			</div>

			<div class="postmetadata clearfix">
        <p class="commentslink alignright">
          <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/2009/07/1-reason-to-stick-with-windows-virtual-pc/#comments" title="Comment on 1 reason to stick with Windows Virtual PC">2 comments</a> &#187;
        </p>
			  <p class="categories">
          Posted in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/category/os/" title="View all posts in OS" rel="category tag">OS</a>,  <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/category/performance/" title="View all posts in Performance" rel="category tag">Performance</a>,  <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/category/powershell/" title="View all posts in Powershell" rel="category tag">Powershell</a>,  <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/category/virtual-pc/" title="View all posts in Virtual PC" rel="category tag">Virtual PC</a>        </p>
